Date: Wed, 14 Jan 2026 09:12:00 -0800
Subject: Re: Stride integration — SDK routing & resiliency kickoff (samples + migration plan)

Hi all — attaching a short implementation pack below and a couple of concrete recommendations we use for customers with similar traffic profiles. If anything needs to be moved into a separate runbook before the kickoff, tell me and I will create it.

Quick recommendations (summary):
- SDK defaults: connection timeout = 2000ms, read/response timeout = 30000ms. Max overall request timeout = 60s for single-call flows.
- Retries: 3 attempts max, exponential backoff with full jitter: delay = random_between(0, base * 2^attempt) with base=200ms. Do not retry on 400/401. Retry on 429/502/503/504. For 429 prefer a linear backoff with Retry-After header respect if present.
- Rate handling: respect per-route X-RateLimit-Remaining and X-RateLimit-Reset headers. If Remaining <= 3, switch to token-bucket smoothing for outgoing requests and slow-start the batch window.
- Batching & KV-cache: enable small-batch continuous batching (batch size 8–16) for standard user flows; pre-warm KV cache for common prefixes during canary 24h before cutover to avoid cold-start latency spikes.
- Endpoint migration: dual-write for 48–72 hours, DNS TTL <= 60s during canary, canary percentages: 5% (1 hour stability), 25% (4 hours), 75% (overnight), 100% once no regressions for 24h. Rollback if error rate > 1.5x baseline or median p95 latency increases > 30%.

Node.js sample (minimal):

const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood.Client({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_KEY });

async function callModel(payload) {
  try {
    const res = await client.generate({
      model: 'rw-large-v1',
      prompt: payload,
      timeout: 30000 // ms
    });
    return res;
  } catch (err) {
    // lightweight retry wrapper: only retry on idempotent errors per rules above
    throw err;
  }
}

Example retry pseudocode (client-side):
- for attempt in 0..2:
    - call with timeout
    - if success -> break
    - if status in [502,503,504] or (429 and no Retry-After) -> wait random_between(0, 200 * 2^attempt) ms and retry
    - if 429 with Retry-After -> respect Retry-After header then retry once

Compliance note: redact PII fields before sending (we suggest using a client-side filter on known field names and a server-side redact-hook during canary).

